 {"id":"CH-CTX","title":"NOTE: the incumbent harness manages its context window \u2014 cap turns, compact via subagents; ours is 6 notes / 300 chars","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Steal from the incumbents, and note this is CTX-WIN's missing half. Continual Harness caps the orchestrator's short-term memory at 20 TURNS and has looping subagents return compacted summaries so inner steps do not consume the window; its checkpoints restore disk state (objectives, long-term memory, trajectories) rather than an in-memory conversation. CONTRAST WITH THIS REPO, measured: the decision path is a fresh two-message request every cycle (cron_runner.py:2115, messages=[{system},{user}] and nothing else); what survives between cycles is _mem_notes capped at 6 entries joined and truncated to 300 characters, plus one _mem_goal string (cron_runner.py:~4502). Prior turns reach the TEACHER request only (cron_runner.py:1409). CTX-WIN is marked complete but its judge verdict was INCOMPLETE on input budget, so treat it as unverified and this as the reference implementation. Done-when: the decision request carries a bounded, managed window of prior turns (with a compaction path for older ones) and the agent can answer a question about a cycle >N back. Est 2 days.","created_at":"2026-09-27T17:27:57.119551+00:00"}
 {"id":"CH-MCP","title":"NOTE: expose the game over MCP so an external agent \u2014 including Hermes \u2014 can drive it","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"depends_on":["CH-SPLIT"],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Steal from the incumbents; this is the highest-leverage integration idea in their design. Continual Harness's run_cli.py 'starts the FastAPI game server on --port, the frame stream server on --port + 1, and an MCP proxy on --mcp-sse-port (default --port + 2) for containerized CLI agents', and its listed external CLI harnesses are Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex, and HERMES. Their PokeAgent docs describe 'a restricted MCP surface' for those external agents, and an 'autonomous_cli' scaffold. OPPORTUNITY FOR THIS REPO: a documented MCP surface (observe + act + memory, restricted tool set) would let this project's environment be driven by any MCP client instead of only its own 5,586-line runner \u2014 turning the harness from a monolith into an attachable environment, and letting us measure different agents against one game state. Depends on CH-SPLIT. Done-when: a documented MCP surface exists with a deliberately restricted tool set, and one end-to-end run is driven by an external MCP client with the transcript recorded. Est 3-4 days. Depends on CH-SPLIT.","created_at":"2026-09-27T17:27:57.119551+00:00"}
